```c
int fd[2];
pipe(fd);              // fd[0] = read end, fd[1] = write end

pid_t pid = fork();
if (pid == 0) {
    close(fd[1]);                   // child only reads
    char buf[100];
    read(fd[0], buf, sizeof(buf));
    printf("child got: %s\n", buf);
} else {
    close(fd[0]);                   // parent only writes
    write(fd[1], "hello", 6);
}
```

Both processes inherit *both* ends of the pipe from `fork()` — closing the end you're not using is essential, not just tidy. If the writer doesn't close its unused read end, a reader waiting for end-of-stream (a `read()` returning 0) can block forever, since the kernel still sees a writer that could theoretically write more. This exact bug — leaving a pipe end open by mistake — is a common real cause of shell scripts or subprocess wrappers that hang forever.
